WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.522 --> 00:00:02.663
- It takes people to
(audio cuts out) hate it

00:00:02.663 --> 00:00:05.163
at the same time, to
make any sort of change

00:00:05.163 --> 00:00:06.392
to what they're doing.

00:00:06.392 --> 00:00:08.185
Of course you want to
have a good reception,

00:00:08.185 --> 00:00:10.146
but you don't make a movie by committee.

00:00:10.146 --> 00:00:11.712
Last take was really good.

00:00:11.712 --> 00:00:13.386
We had that little moment, didn't we?

00:00:13.386 --> 00:00:16.419
Seeing other actors who stand
and somebody shouts action

00:00:16.419 --> 00:00:19.008
and they just go, "I
don't know what to do".

00:00:19.008 --> 00:00:21.664
I've never particularly been
asked to do transformations,

00:00:21.664 --> 00:00:23.727
but, you know, you just
get it in your head.

00:00:23.727 --> 00:00:25.309
Then, you just go, "I need to".

00:00:25.309 --> 00:00:26.936
I'm very myopic in what I choose.

00:00:26.936 --> 00:00:27.995
I don't have any strategy.

00:00:27.995 --> 00:00:28.881
I don't have any plans,

00:00:28.881 --> 00:00:32.619
and then a horrible moment,
when you see a director going,

00:00:32.619 --> 00:00:36.075
"God", you know, lookin' at
you like, "Oh, Jesus Christ".

00:00:36.075 --> 00:00:39.118
- He's an English actor known
for going to great lengths

00:00:39.118 --> 00:00:41.344
to portray characters in his films.

00:00:41.344 --> 00:00:43.259
He first caught the
attention of the public eye

00:00:43.259 --> 00:00:46.409
when he was 13, and he was
cast in the starring role

00:00:46.409 --> 00:00:48.194
of Empire of the Sun.

00:00:48.194 --> 00:00:49.945
He went on to receive greater recognition

00:00:49.945 --> 00:00:53.983
and commercial success with
his role as Bruce Wayne/Batman.

00:00:53.983 --> 00:00:55.880
He's Christian Bale, and here's my take

00:00:55.880 --> 00:00:58.547
on his top 10 rules for success.

00:01:00.984 --> 00:01:02.655
- [Interviewer] Is there a
reason that you get out of bed

00:01:02.655 --> 00:01:05.405
to go act every year when you do?

00:01:07.082 --> 00:01:08.890
- 'Cause when it's good, it's really good.

00:01:08.890 --> 00:01:11.328
When it's not, I (audio
cuts out) hate it, you know,

00:01:11.328 --> 00:01:14.520
but when it's good,
it's a love-hate thing.

00:01:14.520 --> 00:01:15.735
And it's a love-hate thing for me

00:01:15.735 --> 00:01:18.031
because of coming from a place

00:01:18.031 --> 00:01:21.676
where it was actually a
responsibility at a young age,

00:01:21.676 --> 00:01:24.805
because, you know, for my family,

00:01:24.805 --> 00:01:28.801
it was just such an unfathomable
amount of sudden cash

00:01:28.801 --> 00:01:32.046
that you could get for
doing it that I went,

00:01:32.046 --> 00:01:35.267
"Yeah, well even though I don't
really feel like doing it,

00:01:35.267 --> 00:01:38.308
"I can't really live with myself and not,

00:01:38.308 --> 00:01:41.370
"because it helps my family so much,"

00:01:41.370 --> 00:01:44.939
and I felt responsibility to do that.

00:01:44.939 --> 00:01:49.766
I come at it from a slightly
different point of view.

00:01:49.766 --> 00:01:52.173
You know, it wasn't something that I said,

00:01:52.173 --> 00:01:53.216
I went through school going,

00:01:53.216 --> 00:01:54.598
"Oh, you know what? I'd
like to become an actor.

00:01:54.598 --> 00:01:55.575
"Oh yeah, I love films.

00:01:55.575 --> 00:01:57.537
"That's what I'm going to try to do".

00:01:57.537 --> 00:01:59.868
It was something that
happened at an early age,

00:01:59.868 --> 00:02:02.270
and then I went, "I
can't get out of this,"

00:02:02.270 --> 00:02:06.485
because it's really helpful to my family,

00:02:06.485 --> 00:02:07.985
you know, and, so,

00:02:09.569 --> 00:02:11.115
yeah, no, I must do it.

00:02:11.115 --> 00:02:13.221
That's why I've got this
slight love-hate thing with it,

00:02:13.221 --> 00:02:16.129
but I actually think
that love-hate is quite

00:02:16.129 --> 00:02:17.424
a healthy thing to have.

00:02:17.424 --> 00:02:21.575
If all you do is love film,
if all you do is love acting,

00:02:21.575 --> 00:02:24.107
you're just going to imitate
what you love, right?

00:02:24.107 --> 00:02:26.099
It takes people to
(audio cuts out) hate it

00:02:26.099 --> 00:02:28.394
at the same time, to
make any sort of change

00:02:28.394 --> 00:02:30.410
to what they're doing,

00:02:30.410 --> 00:02:34.160
and to me that's when
it becomes interesting.

00:02:35.106 --> 00:02:36.229
- [Narrator] Bale's now been on our screen

00:02:36.229 --> 00:02:37.642
since he was a little boy,

00:02:37.642 --> 00:02:40.233
since Spielberg's Empire of the Sun.

00:02:40.233 --> 00:02:41.066
- Go!

00:02:41.066 --> 00:02:43.210
I love the experience of doing it,

00:02:43.210 --> 00:02:44.669
even though it's kind of strange.

00:02:44.669 --> 00:02:46.682
I was 12 years old, and I
was off in these countries

00:02:46.682 --> 00:02:49.396
by myself, and then, yeah, so I loved,

00:02:49.396 --> 00:02:51.493
but then you get all the press
you have to do afterwards,

00:02:51.493 --> 00:02:53.418
and that kind of did
in my head a little bit

00:02:53.418 --> 00:02:54.376
and made me go, "Yeah, forget it.

00:02:54.376 --> 00:02:55.284
"This ain't for me".

00:02:55.284 --> 00:02:56.617
- That and still now?

00:02:56.617 --> 00:02:59.443
- Always, yeah, yeah,
but that's all right.

00:02:59.443 --> 00:03:01.653
The reason I love being an actor

00:03:01.653 --> 00:03:03.361
is because I'm not playing myself.

00:03:03.361 --> 00:03:04.969
That's the only thing I
know how to do as well.

00:03:04.969 --> 00:03:06.182
(laughs)

00:03:06.182 --> 00:03:07.345
There is that, too.

00:03:07.345 --> 00:03:08.386
I left school really early,

00:03:08.386 --> 00:03:10.501
and I never paid attention
anyways. (laughs)

00:03:10.501 --> 00:03:11.976
- This isn't a career of last resort

00:03:11.976 --> 00:03:12.989
- They want to know.

00:03:12.989 --> 00:03:13.822
It's the only choice.

00:03:13.822 --> 00:03:15.421
There is nothing else.

00:03:15.421 --> 00:03:17.787
I'm not good at anything else.

00:03:17.787 --> 00:03:20.620
When it comes to pleasing people, listen,

00:03:20.620 --> 00:03:22.538
of course you want to
have a good reception,

00:03:22.538 --> 00:03:24.832
but, you don't make a movie by committee,

00:03:24.832 --> 00:03:27.679
and I have to make a movie
that I would want to see,

00:03:27.679 --> 00:03:29.895
and I have to create a character
that I am interested in,

00:03:29.895 --> 00:03:31.157
and that's it.

00:03:31.157 --> 00:03:33.481
I put my head down, and I focus on that,

00:03:33.481 --> 00:03:36.351
and I don't really pay
attention to what people

00:03:36.351 --> 00:03:37.646
may be requesting,

00:03:37.646 --> 00:03:39.697
because I believe that, if
we have enough conviction

00:03:39.697 --> 00:03:42.299
in what we're doing, then
people will believe it as well.

00:03:42.299 --> 00:03:44.315
My belief is that technique should change

00:03:44.315 --> 00:03:47.537
for every single film that you make,

00:03:47.537 --> 00:03:51.326
that you can have, you can get into ruts

00:03:51.326 --> 00:03:54.741
that can be bad ruts
where nothing's working,

00:03:54.741 --> 00:03:56.811
but equally when everything's working;

00:03:56.811 --> 00:03:58.139
that's a rut as well,

00:03:58.139 --> 00:04:00.524
and you have to get out of it.

00:04:00.524 --> 00:04:01.909
You have to just abandon it,

00:04:01.909 --> 00:04:05.479
because it starts to
become phony if you go in,

00:04:05.479 --> 00:04:07.272
well this worked first before,

00:04:07.272 --> 00:04:08.693
and it can be from film to film

00:04:08.693 --> 00:04:10.045
or it can be from take to take.

00:04:10.045 --> 00:04:10.878
Last take was really good.

00:04:10.878 --> 00:04:13.611
We had that little moment, didn't we?

00:04:13.611 --> 00:04:15.348
You said that, and I went (mumbles),

00:04:15.348 --> 00:04:17.373
and you went (mumbles), and
then it (audio cuts out)

00:04:17.373 --> 00:04:19.289
was great, and everyone went ah!

00:04:19.289 --> 00:04:20.476
Let's do that again.

00:04:20.476 --> 00:04:22.194
It'll never be the same.

00:04:22.194 --> 00:04:24.860
You actually have to try to make sure,

00:04:24.860 --> 00:04:27.816
no, ignore that, blow it apart,

00:04:27.816 --> 00:04:29.901
and do something totally different.

00:04:29.901 --> 00:04:30.734
Hopefully, they got that on camera.

00:04:30.734 --> 00:04:33.176
That's a choice they have now.

00:04:33.176 --> 00:04:34.659
Do something completely different

00:04:34.659 --> 00:04:37.716
and, a similar thing, we're
going from one film to the next.

00:04:37.716 --> 00:04:39.274
If you're working with the same director,

00:04:39.274 --> 00:04:42.072
don't fall back on your old
tricks from that old film.

00:04:42.072 --> 00:04:43.601
That was a different film.

00:04:43.601 --> 00:04:45.705
Different tricks were needed,

00:04:45.705 --> 00:04:46.974
Go for new tricks.

00:04:46.974 --> 00:04:48.620
Don't be a one trick pony.

00:04:48.620 --> 00:04:50.122
- [Interviewer] You do
very strong characters

00:04:50.122 --> 00:04:51.875
in pretty much everything.

00:04:51.875 --> 00:04:53.083
You have very strong decisions.

00:04:53.083 --> 00:04:56.029
Do you make those
decisions before you start

00:04:56.029 --> 00:04:58.953
and then come to the set
with what you're going to do

00:04:58.953 --> 00:05:01.759
and then adjust in
small ways, or you what?

00:05:01.759 --> 00:05:03.866
- The way I always approach it is this,

00:05:03.866 --> 00:05:08.033
that I like to have my own
ideas completely worked out,

00:05:09.249 --> 00:05:11.166
so that, if a director,

00:05:12.161 --> 00:05:14.348
I come to find his working method

00:05:14.348 --> 00:05:17.348
is that he doesn't want to talk to me,

00:05:18.491 --> 00:05:20.687
that I'm not at a loss.

00:05:20.687 --> 00:05:21.520
There's nothing worse.

00:05:21.520 --> 00:05:23.477
I've seen other actors who stand,

00:05:23.477 --> 00:05:25.610
and somebody shouts action,
and they're just going,

00:05:25.610 --> 00:05:27.340
"I don't know what to do".

00:05:27.340 --> 00:05:30.986
They need somebody to come
and tell them what to do.

00:05:30.986 --> 00:05:33.678
I never look at a director
as somebody who tells me

00:05:33.678 --> 00:05:34.511
what to do.

00:05:34.511 --> 00:05:38.600
I look at it as someone who
we work together in something,

00:05:38.600 --> 00:05:41.570
and, therefore, I need
to be responsible, too,

00:05:41.570 --> 00:05:44.153
so I have to bring something to the table,

00:05:44.153 --> 00:05:46.358
and say I will do it this way.

00:05:46.358 --> 00:05:48.051
If you never say it, if you keep silent,

00:05:48.051 --> 00:05:49.288
this is how I'll do it,

00:05:49.288 --> 00:05:50.888
and I'll give a few different versions,

00:05:50.888 --> 00:05:55.309
but it would be, it wouldn't
matter if they said nothing.

00:05:55.309 --> 00:05:57.925
I've never particularly been
asked to do transformations,

00:05:57.925 --> 00:06:00.062
but, you know, you just go,
you just get it in your head.

00:06:00.062 --> 00:06:01.529
Then, you just go, "I need to".

00:06:01.529 --> 00:06:02.706
That's what I need to do.

00:06:02.706 --> 00:06:05.335
I don't know how to play that
part without looking that way.

00:06:05.335 --> 00:06:07.063
I always talk with the director about it.

00:06:07.063 --> 00:06:09.913
It's just that they don't
necessarily understand

00:06:09.913 --> 00:06:12.578
quite to what lengths I
might be talking about.

00:06:12.578 --> 00:06:13.882
There's no point in doing something

00:06:13.882 --> 00:06:17.183
if you're not going to do it absolutely.

00:06:17.183 --> 00:06:18.683
Do it to the hilt.

00:06:21.356 --> 00:06:23.526
Yeah, there did come a point
when I suddenly started

00:06:23.526 --> 00:06:26.693
to get more satisfaction
out of it and realizing

00:06:26.693 --> 00:06:29.653
there was something that, even though

00:06:29.653 --> 00:06:34.234
I can't quite bring myself to
say something of worth to it,

00:06:34.234 --> 00:06:38.151
that I can say something
worth me doing it for,

00:06:39.665 --> 00:06:41.571
and, if I'm going to do it,
if I'm going to take the time

00:06:41.571 --> 00:06:43.904
to do it, then I want to do it

00:06:44.794 --> 00:06:48.582
in as much of an
immersive way as possible,

00:06:48.582 --> 00:06:49.776
because you're just coming to learn

00:06:49.776 --> 00:06:53.458
in life that the things that
you really get a kick out of,

00:06:53.458 --> 00:06:56.565
the things that you just can't deny,

00:06:56.565 --> 00:06:59.228
they're the things that,
when you're doing them,

00:06:59.228 --> 00:07:00.870
you can think of nothing else

00:07:00.870 --> 00:07:02.862
but what you're doing, you know?

00:07:02.862 --> 00:07:05.202
I (audio cuts out) up
my whole arm and wrist.

00:07:05.202 --> 00:07:07.568
I got all metal plates
here and here and screws

00:07:07.568 --> 00:07:10.194
all up and down my arm
through riding motorcycles,

00:07:10.194 --> 00:07:13.139
not on the streets, but on racetracks,

00:07:13.139 --> 00:07:17.306
and that was something which
I realized was very much

00:07:18.994 --> 00:07:21.361
when acting is good, why I like it,

00:07:21.361 --> 00:07:25.374
because, when you're riding a motorcycle,

00:07:25.374 --> 00:07:26.374
to the hilt,

00:07:27.442 --> 00:07:29.530
knowing the danger involved,

00:07:29.530 --> 00:07:33.102
you have to be absolutely
focused on what you're doing.

00:07:33.102 --> 00:07:34.641
You can't allow anything else in,

00:07:34.641 --> 00:07:37.502
and there is such a thrill
to doing that, you know.

00:07:37.502 --> 00:07:40.373
That's why it's so hypnotic
and just mesmerizing

00:07:40.373 --> 00:07:41.812
and I just love it,

00:07:41.812 --> 00:07:44.562
and I realize that the best times

00:07:45.517 --> 00:07:47.711
I feel a similar thing with acting,

00:07:47.711 --> 00:07:49.735
and it doesn't happen that often,

00:07:49.735 --> 00:07:51.368
because it's a team thing.

00:07:51.368 --> 00:07:53.656
On a motorcycle, it's
just you and it going.

00:07:53.656 --> 00:07:56.518
This is a team thing, and
you require the environment

00:07:56.518 --> 00:07:58.619
to be created in which you can do that,

00:07:58.619 --> 00:08:02.209
but, when that does happen,
that's what I love about it.

00:08:02.209 --> 00:08:04.997
It was in talking with Adam,

00:08:04.997 --> 00:08:07.605
and hearing just, you know,

00:08:07.605 --> 00:08:11.522
man this guy was so
obsessed with this subject,

00:08:14.026 --> 00:08:17.783
and I want to work with
people who are obsessed

00:08:17.783 --> 00:08:19.061
with what they're doing, you know.

00:08:19.061 --> 00:08:23.686
I don't want to work with
anyone who's just sort of

00:08:23.686 --> 00:08:25.712
phoning it in, and, oh
yeah, it's all right.

00:08:25.712 --> 00:08:26.916
I could do it or do something else.

00:08:26.916 --> 00:08:29.187
I want someone who's kind
of, I got to do this film,

00:08:29.187 --> 00:08:30.057
I got to do this film.

00:08:30.057 --> 00:08:31.918
That's very attractive.

00:08:31.918 --> 00:08:34.776
You wanted me: here I am.

00:08:34.776 --> 00:08:36.468
I'm very myopic in what I choose.

00:08:36.468 --> 00:08:38.567
I don't have any strategy,
I don't have any plans,

00:08:38.567 --> 00:08:40.576
I don't have any particular kind of film

00:08:40.576 --> 00:08:42.403
that I can articulate that I like.

00:08:42.403 --> 00:08:45.554
I just look at each day very up close,

00:08:45.554 --> 00:08:46.934
what's in front of me.

00:08:46.934 --> 00:08:47.901
Do I like it?

00:08:47.901 --> 00:08:48.884
Does it stick with me?

00:08:48.884 --> 00:08:50.276
Could I work on it for a few months?

00:08:50.276 --> 00:08:51.109
That's it.

00:08:51.109 --> 00:08:54.131
The slightest amount
of control that I have

00:08:54.131 --> 00:08:56.531
is what happens on the actual set.

00:08:56.531 --> 00:08:59.095
You sometimes get really
great scenes that are cut.

00:08:59.095 --> 00:09:01.173
That happens, you know, so what do you do?

00:09:01.173 --> 00:09:02.796
You say to yourself, "All right, well,

00:09:02.796 --> 00:09:04.245
"let's get the satisfaction

00:09:04.245 --> 00:09:05.542
"out of actually making it".

00:09:05.542 --> 00:09:06.712
That's the only way.

00:09:06.712 --> 00:09:09.071
It's the only way to get
any satisfaction from it.

00:09:09.071 --> 00:09:11.159
The rest of it is all gravy.

00:09:11.159 --> 00:09:12.616
- [Interviewer] But it must
be always be a great moment

00:09:12.616 --> 00:09:14.945
when you see the light
in the director's eyes,

00:09:14.945 --> 00:09:16.772
seeing something they didn't expect,

00:09:16.772 --> 00:09:17.828
when you're on that set.

00:09:17.828 --> 00:09:18.661
- Oh, that's great.

00:09:18.661 --> 00:09:19.494
Oh, of course, yeah yeah yeah,

00:09:19.494 --> 00:09:21.118
when they go, "Oh yeah,
I didn't notice that,"

00:09:21.118 --> 00:09:21.951
and then a horrible moment

00:09:21.951 --> 00:09:25.150
when you see a director going,

00:09:25.150 --> 00:09:28.217
"God," you know, lookin' at
you like, "Oh, Jesus Christ,"

00:09:28.217 --> 00:09:29.701
or actually saying that.

00:09:29.701 --> 00:09:31.851
"Jesus Christ, what was that,"

00:09:31.851 --> 00:09:32.929
(laughs)

00:09:32.929 --> 00:09:35.752
or walking past you and
just going. (audio cuts out)

00:09:35.752 --> 00:09:36.772
It's like what, and you go,

00:09:36.772 --> 00:09:38.458
"What the (audio cuts out) was that".

00:09:38.458 --> 00:09:39.291
Like, "What".

00:09:39.291 --> 00:09:40.124
You're not even going to explain

00:09:40.124 --> 00:09:42.291
why you're swearing at me?

00:09:43.293 --> 00:09:44.167
But that's what I enjoy.

00:09:44.167 --> 00:09:45.000
I enjoy that.

00:09:45.000 --> 00:09:47.799
I enjoy all the different
relationships that you have.

00:09:47.799 --> 00:09:48.773
- [Interviewer] It sounds
like it's about as good

00:09:48.773 --> 00:09:51.940
as it can get right now, it sounds like.

00:09:51.940 --> 00:09:53.966
Did you find interesting
things in doing the work

00:09:53.966 --> 00:09:56.029
the way you need to do it and--

00:09:56.029 --> 00:09:58.455
- Yeah, but pride comes
before a fall, doesn't it?

00:09:58.455 --> 00:10:00.354
I mean, you get, you never know, you know.

00:10:00.354 --> 00:10:01.996
I mean that's what I do enjoy.

00:10:01.996 --> 00:10:06.627
You just really never know
and so often the films

00:10:06.627 --> 00:10:10.272
that people think you should
do and are the right moves

00:10:10.272 --> 00:10:12.864
and seem to be great,
it's too good to be true

00:10:12.864 --> 00:10:17.133
for a reason, and you end
up going, "Yeah, it's okay".

00:10:17.133 --> 00:10:18.159
That's it, you know,

00:10:18.159 --> 00:10:22.272
and, so often, what I
found, in my own career,

00:10:22.272 --> 00:10:24.439
if you want to call it that,

00:10:25.345 --> 00:10:28.433
is it's the films where people
go, "There's a big chance

00:10:28.433 --> 00:10:30.828
"that this is going to go
south," and I tend to go,

